<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fukuoka</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fukuoka is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka-prefecture">Fukuoka Prefecture</a>, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakata">Hakata</a> Bay facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. The area was an early gateway for contacts with the continent, from the Yayoi-period state of Na to the medieval port of Hakata. The castle town of Fukuoka was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuroda-nagamasa">Kuroda Nagamasa</a> in 1601 and merged with Hakata in 1889 to form the modern city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fukuoka.jpg" alt="Fukuoka" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nryate · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fukuoka_Skyline_of_Seaside_Momochi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Fukuoka is built along Hakata Bay, bordered by mountains on three sides and open to the Genkai Sea on the north. It is the point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s main islands nearest to the Asian mainland, and Busan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> lies about 180 kilometres away. People have lived in the area since the Jomon period, and settlers of the Yayoi period were among the earliest to arrive. Distance from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> and later Edo gave the city a distinctive local culture and dialect.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-later-han">Book of the Later Han</a> records that an envoy from the region visited the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-guangwu-of-han">Emperor Guangwu</a> in 57 CE and received a gold seal, which was found in the 17th century. By the end of the Asuka period the port, sometimes called the Port of Dazaifu, had grown into the regional government headquarters for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> and a trade centre with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>. Trade continued to grow after the Kōrokan declined at the end of the Heian period, and monks who had studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> founded temples there. Eisai founded Shōfuku-ji, described as the oldest Zen temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and is credited with bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> to Japan; Enni, who built Joten-ji, is credited with bringing udon from China.</p><p>From 1268 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> sent envoys to make the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> shogunate acknowledge his suzerainty, and the shogunate refused. During the Muromachi period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakata">Hakata</a> was the first free city in Japanese history, governed by a council of 12 wealthy merchants called Tongyoji, and it remained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s main port for trade with mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, particularly with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and Yuan dynasties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. The name Fukuoka, combining the characters for good fortune and hill, was given by the daimyō <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuroda-nagamasa">Kuroda Nagamasa</a> to his new castle town after the Fukuoka district of Bizen Province, the ancestral home of his clan.</p><p>In the early 20th century the city gained the Fukuoka Medical College in 1903 and a streetcar service in 1910. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> it was a regional military centre, and it was firebombed on 19 June 1945, an attack that destroyed 21.5 per cent of its urban area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Fukuoka is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka-prefecture">Fukuoka Prefecture</a> and the most populous city on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, with a population of over a million and a half in seven wards. It was designated a city by government ordinance on 1 April 1972. Its oldest festival, Hakata Gion Yamakasa, is held for two weeks each July and is said to date from 1241. The city museum displays the gold seal of 57 CE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fukuoka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Between 1996 and 1999 a team led by Satoshi Yamaguchi of Japan’s National Museum of Nature and Science also found many similarities between Yayoi remains from Yamaguchi and <b>Fukuoka</b> prefectures and remains from Jiangsu province on the Chinese coast.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yayoi-culture">Yayoi culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>He built <b>Fukuoka</b> castle beside the old merchant city of Hakata.</blockquote><a href="/state/fukuoka-domain">Fukuoka Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Na grew up in the <b>Fukuoka</b> plain of northern Kyushu during the Yayoi period, when wet rice farming and metalworking spread from Korea.</blockquote><a href="/state/na-japan">Na (Japan)</a></li><li><blockquote>Saga shared guard duty at Nagasaki with <b>Fukuoka</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/saga-domain">Saga Domain</a></li></ul></section>
